MoBetta: x87sidecar: Bridge movzx/movsx from 8/16-bit memory and widen the gap limits.

Two things kept x87 runs apart in WoW 3.3.5a after 0002-0004:

- Gaps longer than X87_BRIDGE_MAX_GAP (4).  WoW's hottest x87 function
  (0x97c001, 15% of Wow.exe samples at the Dalaran fountain) copies a
  3-float vector from a global between x87 ops: `mov edx,[0xb2d540]; mov
  eax,[esi+4]; mov [eax],edx; ...`, 8-10 plain movs.  Every one of them was
  already a bridge shape (an absolute address decodes as a MemRef with no
  base register), so only the gap limit kept the runs apart.  The bounds go
  to [1,16] / [1,32] and the defaults to gap 12 / total 24.

- movzx/movsx r32/r64 from an 8- or 16-bit memory operand.  New IR op
  BridgeLoadExt: LDRB/LDRH zero-extend into the W register, LDRSB/LDRSH
  sign-extend to the destination width (W form for r32, which clears bits
  63:32 as the register form's SBFX does).  The node's imm_bits shares
  storage with mem_operand, so the sign flag rides negatively encoded in
  inputs[1] and the width comes from the operand.  Flag-free; a barrier
  for load sinking and fenced off DSE and fma_reduce like BridgeLoadG.
  movsxd r64,m32 and segment overrides stay with stock.

Offline, Session #28 Dalaran profile (profile_analyze, production config):
19.51 -> 18.82 ARM per x87 op (-3.5%; wider limits alone 19.00, movzx
alone 19.34), no IR build, FPR or GPR failures.  Gap 12 / total 32 gives
18.79; 24 keeps regions shorter for the same result.

A memory fault inside a joined region behaves as for any bridged load or
store today (DECISIONS.md #22); wider gaps put more plain loads and stores
in such regions.

Tests: tests/test_bridge_loadext.c (movzx/movsx m8/m16 into r32 with
garbage upper halves and into r64, values 0x80/0x7f/0xff/0x8000/0xffff, a
loaded byte as the next x87 op's index, a ten-instruction gap of loads and
stores read back by x87; all four regions logged as joined); suite 1486
passed, 0 failed, 2 known stock divergences.  MoBetta tests/x87_bridge_stack.c
under Wine: bits 8c4b97bdea0f82c5 as before, loop 305 -> 273 ms (median of
5); tests/x87_bridge_shift.c bits a2d1d8e50f5de46d, 174 -> 172 ms.

